WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 243: John Marshall: The Great Chief Justice, Marbury v. Madison, and the Rule of Law

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.000
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:22.640
[Rebecca] Today, our chronicle of the top 50 famous non-president Americans follows the man who single-handedly forged the American legal order, establishing the Supreme Court as the supreme guardian of constitutional liberty, Chief Justice John Marshall.

3
00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:36.720
[George] Born in 1755 in a humble two-room log cabin in the Blue Ridge frontier of Virginia, John Marshall was the eldest of 15 children, raised on the rugged edge of civilization.

4
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:47.920
[George] Tall, gangly, and athletic, he roamed the pine forests in fringed buckskins, listening to his father Thomas Marshall read aloud from Alexander Pope and William Blackstone.

5
00:00:47.920 --> 00:01:00.720
[George] When the Revolutionary War erupted, 19-year-old Marshall enlisted as a lieutenant in the Culpepper Minutemen, marching beneath the defiant yellow banner bearing a coiled rattlesnake and the motto, Don't Tread on Me.

6
00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:12.720
[George] At Brandywine, Germantown, and during the freezing crucible of Valley Forge in the winter of 1777, Marshall endured the bitter cold and starvation alongside George Washington.

7
00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:28.480
[George] While other officers complained, Marshall's infectious good humor, optimism, and unyielding fortitude sustained the spirits of the shivering soldiers, earning him the affectionate nickname, the Silver Heeled Runner for his legendary athletic jumping feats.

8
00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:43.600
[George] Valley Forge forged Marshall's lifelong political philosophy, watching soldiers freeze and starve while sovereign state legislatures refused to send shoes, flour, or pay convinced him that a weak, fragmented confederation was suicidal.

9
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:48.800
[George] Instilling in him a fierce devotion to an energetic, sovereign American Union.

10
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:59.840
[George] Marshall's frontier upbringing instilled in him a lifelong disdain for aristocratic pretension, wearing simple homespun linen and drinking cider with ordinary farmers in Richmond taverns.

11
00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:10.360
[George] Marshall's father had fought alongside George Washington in the French and Indian War, instilling in young John an almost religious reverence for Washington's character and leadership.

12
00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:27.560
[George] Following the war, Marshall attended law lectures by George Wyth at William and Mary, established a premier law practice in Richmond, and became the leading Federalist orator defending the new constitution in the Virginia ratifying convention against the passionate eloquence of Patrick Henry.

13
00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:38.360
[George] In 1797, President John Adams appointed Marshall as one of three special envoys to Paris to negotiate an end to the quasiwar against revolutionary France.

14
00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:51.400
[George] Upon arriving in Paris, the American diplomats were secretly approached by three French agents, codenamed X, Y, and Z by American dispatches, representing French Foreign Minister Tolleand.

15
00:02:51.400 --> 00:03:04.600
[George] The agents insolently demanded a massive personal bribe of $150,000, a $12 million loan to France, and a formal American apology before Tollyrand would even agree to receive them.

16
00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:16.600
[George] Marshall refused with cold American dignity, drafting blistering diplomatic responses that dismantled French arrogance and declared that free Americans would never pay tribute to foreign despots.

17
00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:29.160
[George] Returning to Philadelphia, Marshall was greeted as a national hero to the rallying cry of millions for defense, but not one cent for tribute, cementing his reputation as an incorruptible statesman.

18
00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:40.760
[George] In Paris, Marshall kept a daily secret journal recording the corrupt overtures of Tollyrand's emissaries, which President Adams later submitted to Congress to electrify the young nation.

19
00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:53.440
[George] The XYZ affair united Americans behind the slogan Millions for Defense, but not one cent for tribute, leading to the creation of the Department of the Navy and the commissioning of USS Constitution.

20
00:03:53.440 --> 00:04:03.360
[George] In the bitter, contested presidential election of 1800, Thomas Jefferson and the Democratic-Republicans swept the Federalists from the Presidency and Congress.

21
00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:20.160
[George] In the closing weeks of his administration, determined to preserve a Federalist constitutional check against Jeffersonian radicalism, President John Adams nominated his Secretary of State, 45-year-old John Marshall, as the fourth Chief Justice of the United States.

22
00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:35.680
[George] At that time, the Supreme Court was a weak, prestigious and name-only tribunal that met in a cramped, humid committee room in the basement of the Capitol, with no permanent building and having had three chief justices resign in disgust to take state appointments.

23
00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:40.720
[George] Marshall stepped into the office and immediately revolutionized judicial practice.

24
00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:57.360
[George] He abandoned the British tradition of serietum opinions, where each judge delivered his own rambling individual verdict, and persuaded his fellow justices to live together in the same boarding house on Capitol Hill, dining, drinking Madeira wine, and issuing unified.

25
00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:05.280
[George] Unanimous opinions authored by Marshall himself, transforming the court into a coherent, respected national institution.

26
00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:17.840
[George] Marshall's boarding house arrangement on Capitol Hill fostered a remarkable judicial brotherhood, where the justices debated constitutional principles over glasses of wine until achieving unanimous consensus.

27
00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:29.280
[George] Under Marshall's leadership, the Supreme Court struck down state laws that violated the federal constitution in over 60 cases, establishing the definitive supremacy of the Federal Union.

28
00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:36.840
[George] In 1803, Marshall confronted the greatest constitutional showdown in early American history, Marbury v.

29
00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:37.800
[George] Madison.

30
00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:51.160
[George] William Marbury, appointed a federal justice of the peace by outgoing President Adams, sued Secretary of State James Madison to force the delivery of his signed commission, which Jefferson's administration had withheld.

31
00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:54.280
[George] Marshall faced an impossible political trap.

32
00:05:54.280 --> 00:06:03.000
[George] If he ordered Madison to deliver the commission, Jefferson would simply ignore the court's order, exposing the judiciary as toothless and powerless.

33
00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:09.160
[George] If he refused to order delivery, the court would appear to surrender cowardly to presidential bullying.

34
00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:13.160
[George] Marshall engineered a breathtaking masterstroke of legal judo.

35
00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:22.520
[George] Writing for a unanimous court, he ruled that Marbury was indeed legally entitled to his commission and that Jefferson's administration had violated the law.

36
00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:40.120
[George] But then, Marshall declared that the specific congressional statute empowering the Supreme Court to issue the order, Section 13 of the Judiciary Act of 1789, was unconstitutional because Congress could not expand the court's original jurisdiction beyond the text of Article III.

37
00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:57.480
[George] By sacrificing a minor judicial commission, Marshall established the colossal, revolutionary doctrine of judicial review, the principle that the Constitution is the supreme law of the land, and that it is emphatically the province and duty of the judicial department to say what the law is.

38
00:06:57.480 --> 00:07:06.040
[George] Jefferson was left powerless to resist, while Marshall secured the Supreme Court as the co-equal final arbiter of American constitutional law.

39
00:07:06.040 --> 00:07:07.640
[George] In Marbury v.

40
00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:18.480
[George] Madison, Marshall established that the Constitution is not a mere political aspiration, but a permanent, binding legal charter that limits the power of every branch of government.

41
00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:20.960
[George] Marshall's legal reasoning in Marbury v.

42
00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:29.520
[George] Madison was so masterfully balanced that neither Jefferson nor the Republican Congress could find any procedural ground to overturn it.

43
00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:40.480
[George] In 1807, Marshall demonstrated his unyielding defense of civil liberties and the rule of law during the sensational treason trial of former vice president Aaron Burr.

44
00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:50.800
[George] Burr was accused by President Jefferson of organizing a treasonous conspiracy to carve out an independent Western Empire in the Spanish territories and seize New Orleans.

45
00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:59.840
[George] Jefferson used the full power of the presidency to demand Burr's hanging, issuing public declarations of guilt and mobilizing government prosecutors.

46
00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:05.040
[George] Marshall presided over the circuit court trial in Richmond with stoic impartiality.

47
00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:17.280
[George] When prosecutors sought to convict Burr based on loose hearsay and constructive treason, Marshall strictly enforced the narrow, protective definition of treason set down in Article III of the Constitution.

48
00:08:17.280 --> 00:08:27.200
[George] Treason could consist only in levying war against the United States or adhering to their enemies, requiring the testimony of two eyewitnesses to the same overt act.

49
00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:32.080
[George] Because prosecutors could prove no overt military act, Burr was acquitted.

50
00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:44.400
[George] Marshall proved that in a constitutional republic governed by the rule of law, no president, no political mob, and no executive decree could bend the Constitution to execute a political enemy.

51
00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:56.880
[George] During Burr's trial, Jefferson was so outraged by Marshall's strict constitutional standards that he privately threatened to impeach the Chief Justice, but Marshall refused to waver before executive pressure.

52
00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:08.760
[George] In the Burr trial, Marshall issued a subpoena Duches Tecum to President Jefferson demanding executive documents, establishing that the President of the United States is subject to the rule of law.

53
00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:21.080
[George] Over his 34-year tenure, the longest of any Chief Justice in American history, Marshall authored over 500 opinions that laid the bedrock of modern American federal power and economic vitality.

54
00:09:21.080 --> 00:09:22.760
[George] In McCulloch v.

55
00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:38.120
[George] Maryland in 1819, Marshall upheld the constitutionality of the Bank of the United States and struck down Maryland's attempt to tax it, giving life to the necessary and proper clause and declaring that the power to tax involves the power to destroy.

56
00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:40.200
[George] In Dartmouth College v.

57
00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:49.800
[George] Woodward, he protected private contracts from arbitrary state interference, establishing legal security for American corporations and universities.

58
00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:51.160
[George] In Gibbons v.

59
00:09:51.400 --> 00:10:04.520
[George] Ogden in 1824, Marshall dismantled state steamboat monopolies on the Hudson River, interpreting the Commerce Clause broadly to create a unified national internal market free from state trade barriers.

60
00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:13.400
[George] Marshall's jurisprudence transformed a fragile collection of thirteen sovereign states into a single, indivisible continental commercial republic.

61
00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:15.800
[George] Marshall's opinion in McCulloch v.

62
00:10:16.120 --> 00:10:25.720
[George] Maryland was so controversial in the South that the Chief Justice published anonymous defense essays in the Richmond Inquirer under the pseudonym, A Friend of the Union.

63
00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:26.840
[George] Gibbons v.

64
00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:38.120
[George] Ogden unlocked the economic potential of American rivers and railroads, eliminating state tariffs and creating the world's largest free trade common market within the United States.

65
00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:49.920
[George] In his final years, Marshall stood as the last surviving titan of the revolutionary generation, revered across political divides for his warmth, simplicity, and intellectual brilliance.

66
00:10:49.920 --> 00:11:02.880
[George] Even in old age, the Chief Justice walked miles through Washington every morning in an unbuttoned threadbare coat, buying fresh strawberries in the market and playing coits on Saturdays with local bricklayers and lawyers.

67
00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:03.920
[George] In Worcester v.

68
00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:18.240
[George] Georgia in 1832, Marshall delivered a courageous defense of the Cherokee Nation, ruling that state laws could have no force within sovereign Cherokee lands, prompting President Andrew Jackson's famous, apocryphal challenge.

69
00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:20.720
[George] John Marshall has made his decision.

70
00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:22.560
[George] Now let him enforce it.

71
00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:28.880
[George] Marshall died in Philadelphia on July 6, 1835, at the age of 79.

72
00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:41.040
[George] According to Philadelphia tradition, as the city told the Liberty Bell in the Tower of Independence Hall during his funeral procession, the ancient bronze bell cracked open, falling silent forever.

73
00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:52.720
[George] John Marshall took a fragile, untried constitution written on parchment and breathed into it the living institutional authority that preserved the American Republic through centuries of storm and crisis.

74
00:11:52.720 --> 00:12:03.920
[George] Marshall was the principal biographer of George Washington, publishing a monumental five-volume biography celebrating the father of his country whose character had shaped his own life.

75
00:12:03.920 --> 00:12:16.320
[George] John Marshall's bronze statue, sculpted by William Wetmore's story, sits at the foot of the western steps of the United States Capitol, gazing westward toward the continent unified by his law.

76
00:12:20.280 --> 00:12:33.000
[Rebecca] Next week, our top 50 non-president American series continues with episode 244: Henry Clay, The Great Pacificator, The American System, and the Compromise of 1850.

77
00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:35.720
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

